<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Hawaii</h1><p class="st-meta">1894 CE – 1898 CE · capital Honolulu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oligarchic republic proclaimed by the overthrow's leaders in 1894, which held Hawaii until it was annexed by the United States in 1898.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a> refused to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-hawaii">Provisional Government of Hawaii</a> called a constitutional convention. The new constitution was proclaimed on 4 July 1894, creating a republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole">Sanford B. Dole</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution restricted voting through property, income and literacy tests and loyalty oaths, excluding most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a> and all Asian immigrants. Power stayed with the American-descended business elite tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. In January 1895 royalists led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-william-wilcox">Robert Wilcox</a> attempted a counter-revolution, which failed; Liliʻuokalani was arrested, forced to abdicate and imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a>. Japanese and Chinese labour remained central to the plantations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> strategically valuable, and after a treaty failed in the Senate despite petitions by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>, Congress annexed the islands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newlands-resolution">Newlands Resolution</a> in July 1898. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> was transferred on 12 August 1898, and the islands became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-hawaii">Territory of Hawaii</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-hawaii.jpg" alt="Republic of Hawaii" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: FOTW, Clay Moss (PNG), Government of the Republic of Hawaii · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii_(1896).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1894 CE – 1898 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Honolulu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sanford B. Dole</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Hawaiian religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Hawaiian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hawaiian Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=republic-of-hawaii" aria-label="Republic of Hawaii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="439 1390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Hawaii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M759 1646L759 1646M759 1646L2570 1008M759 1646L759 1646"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M759 1646h0M2570 1008h0M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Honolulu. <a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=republic-of-hawaii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
